<comment datatype='string'>Like the cyclohyrolase from methanogenic archaea, the methenyl-H&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;MPT cyclohydrolase from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-272630&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Methylorubrum extorquens&lt;/i&gt; AM1&lt;/a&gt; is specific for H&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;MPT, catalyzes the formation of the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sup&gt;5&lt;/sup&gt;-derivative, and is a monofunctional enzyme [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10215859&quot;&gt;Pomper99&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The enzyme has been purified and characterized. It is composed of two identical subunits of molecular mass 33 kDa [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10215859&quot;&gt;Pomper99&lt;/a&gt;]. The gene encoding the enzyme was identified via the N-terminal amino acid sequence. The sequence is approximately 40% identical to that of the orthologous enzymes from methanogenic archaea [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10215859&quot;&gt;Pomper99&lt;/a&gt;].</comment>
